 "observations": [
  "The page number «156» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 45», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L04), in bold, the sigla in roman: N's title, O's «Tadil elzohara beied hakht eneid» and C's «Tadil elzohara bei ed halt eneid»; L04, the place of the node of Venus, «Locus autem draconis eius 1. 29. 27», in smaller bold type, followed by «(CN)»: one sign 29° 27′, the value of chapter 19 of the text (p. 15, JP2 0047) and of p. 64 (JP2 0096, L38), 59° 27′. The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of L04 and below it, «C 65r, O 122v,» and «N 19v.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on pp. 150-155 (JP2 0182-0187), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Via numeri (C)» over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmentum proprietatum omnium» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium» in smaller type, without notes; «Sublimatio ascensus Veneris examinata1)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza2)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Veneris»; «Latitudo Veneris accedentis3)» over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L05-L09), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L10, the units, in bold.",
  "L11-L40. Checked: each value of the Sublimatio is 2s 21° 15′, the apogee of Venus (text, chapter 18, p. 15, JP2 0047; 81° 15′), less half the Examinatio argumenti of the same row, a half minute counted as a whole, except at 20° and 21° (L30, L31): there 2s 17° 3′ and 2s 16° 50′ are 1′ more than the 2s 17° 2′ and 2s 16° 49′ that the Examinatio argumenti of the rows, 8° 27′ and 8° 52′, requires. The Examinatio argumenti agrees to within 0.9′ in every row with the equation of the anomaly of an epicycle, arc tan (r sin x : (60 + r cos x)), for r = 43.95, the radius that fits the thirty values best; it gives 8° 26.5′ and 8° 51.7′ at 20° and 21°, so that the excess lies in the Sublimatio. No note of the apparatus bears on these two figures.",
  "L11-L40. The Latitudo secunda is 5° · sin x to within 0.6″ in every row except at 1° and 4°, where 0° 5′ 16″ and 0° 20′ 57″ are 1.9″ and 1.4″ more than 0° 5′ 14.1″ and 0° 20′ 55.6″; its greatest value, 5°, is to be compared with p. 158. The thirty values are those of the Latitudo secunda of Saturn (p. 138, JP2 0170), whose greatest latitude is also 5°, except at 1°, 4° and 7°, where Saturn has 0° 5′ 14″, 0° 20′ 56″ and 0° 36′ 33″ (0° 36′ 33.6″ computed, here 0° 36′ 34″).",
  "L11-L40, the other columns, compared from row to row: the Examinatio centri increases by 2′ or 3′ a degree to 1° 7′ at 30°, the half of 2° 14′, the greatest equation of the sun (Tab. 21, p. 132, JP2 0164); it lies between 1.2′ below and 0.3′ above 2° 14′ · sin x. The Statio prima increases from 5s 15° 51′ to 5s 16° 2′ by 1′ every second or third degree. The Latitudo prima is 2° 24′ at 1°, 2° 23′ at 2° and 3°, 2° 22′ to 6°, 2° 21′ to 14°, and then decreases by 1′ every fourth degree to 2° 17′ from 27° on.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 1s 0°, is N's; C and O have 0. Checked: the Sublimatio 2s 20° 50′ at 2° (note b, C, against O's 1) agrees with the rule, 2s 20° 49½′; the Examinatio argumenti 8° 1′ at 19° (note c, O and N, against C's 50) with the Sublimatio of the row, 2s 17° 15′, and with the epicycle, 8° 1.2′; the Examinatio centri 0° 30′ at 13° (note d, C, against O's 19, reported with the editor's question mark) and 0° 37′ at 16° (note e, O and N, against C's 36) with the steps of the column; its degree 1 from 27° to 30° (notes f-f, N, against the 0 of C and O), the minutes passing from 0° 58′ at 26° to 1° 0′ at 27°.",
  "Italics: none in the apparatus. N01: the ellipsis «. . . .» of the lemma as printed, set close to the words on either side; «Sullimatio», C's reading, as on p. 138 (JP2 0170, note 4)."
 ],
